# Documentation Standards

Documentation has **two layers**. Both are required where they apply. They answer different questions and **do not replace each other**.

| Layer | Question it answers | Where |
|-------|---------------------|--------|
| **Public contract** | What does this API do? What goes in and out? | Javadoc on public types and methods |
| **Algorithm** | How does this implementation work step by step? | `//` comments before each logical block inside method bodies |

When the user asks to document code, they mean **both** unless they specify otherwise. Students will read this on a projector — keep both layers plain-language.

## Public Contract (Required on Public API)

- `/**` Javadoc on public types and public methods
- `@param` for each method parameter
- `@return` when the method returns a value
- Keep summaries concise and action-oriented

## Algorithm Comments (Required in Method Bodies)

- Add a brief `//` comment on its own line **before each logical block**
- Cover every distinct step: validation, branching, movement, firing, scanning
- Write action-oriented sentences starting with verbs ("Turn the gun toward the scanned robot")
- Apply to **public and private methods alike** when the body has non-trivial logic
- **Never inline** — comments always sit on their own line above the code they describe
- Skip body comments only for truly trivial one-liners

## What Good Looks Like

```java
/**
 * Fire at a robot that was just scanned.
 *
 * @param event the scan event from Robocode
 */
public void onScannedRobot(ScannedRobotEvent event)
{
    // Turn the gun toward the scanned robot.
    turnGunRight(event.getBearing());

    // Fire a full-power shot.
    fire(3.0);
}
```
